  "slug": "concentrate-contacts-62cb",
  "summary": "Agent benchmarks, cold-start leakage checklists and conflict-aware affinity models converge on making biomedical ML claims auditable and generalizable.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is making biomedical ML claims checkable under distribution shift, rather than raising headline accuracy. A perspective on drug interaction prediction frames cold-start evaluation as auditable claims, with an L0-L5 framework and a leakage checklist. A related paper converts publications into auditable agent benchmarks, with 121 spatial transcriptomics tasks on which agents matched or beat baselines on 80%. The ASD fMRI work constrains an LLM agent to fixed computational tools while it searches 71 pipelines for site-independent classifiers. On the modeling side, MIRAGE-DTA arbitrates conflicting modality evidence using modality masks and training-only retrieval, and FedSPL shares embedding prototypes to address scaffold heterogeneity in federated property prediction. What is new is that generalization, leakage control and reliability are treated as explicit design targets. The items do not address antibody binder design.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
